import{_ as t,o as a,c as s,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.BM5YPQmL.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Known issues","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"root/KNOWN_ISSUES.md","filePath":"root/KNOWN_ISSUES.md"}'),d={name:"root/KNOWN_ISSUES.md"};function o(i,e,n,l,h,c){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="known-issues" tabindex="-1">Known issues <a class="header-anchor" href="#known-issues" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Known issues&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The running record of known defects and their status. The 2.0.0 release shipped with the seventeen issues below (found by the 2.0.0 postcheck, <code>audit/2.0.0-postcheck/</code> in the private mirror); <strong>all seventeen are fixed in 2.1.0</strong>. The v1-era issue archive lives in <code>docs/archive/KNOWN_ISSUES.md</code>.</p><h2 id="_2-0-0-issues-all-fixed-in-2-1-0" tabindex="-1">2.0.0 issues - all fixed in 2.1.0 <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-0-0-issues-all-fixed-in-2-1-0" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2.0.0 issues - all fixed in 2.1.0&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Class</th><th>Issue</th><th>Fixed in 2.1.0 by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P-01</td><td>P0 CI/release gate</td><td>The v2.0.0 release code failed to COMPILE on the CI runners (tui_core.c in/out with an imm8 &quot;Nd&quot; constraint on port 0x3F8/0x3FD - ports &gt;= 0x100 must go through DX). All 15 v2.0.0 CI runs were red; the release shipped local-green/CI-red, undeclared.</td><td>Port I/O forced through <code>%dx</code> (&quot;d&quot; constraint, u16 operand); plus two further CI-environment traps found and fixed on the way: the runner GRUB (2.12+) dropped <code>efi_uga.mod</code> (module lists are now filtered against the installed GRUB), and gcc &gt;= 15 defaults to C23 where <code>typedef u8 bool</code> collides with the native <code>_Bool</code> keyword (core/types.h keeps the compiler&#39;s bool under C23, u8 under gnu11). CI is green again and is a hard release gate.</td></tr><tr><td>P-02</td><td>P1 release data</td><td>Release-note numbers contradicted each other (&quot;134 of the 251 commands moved&quot; vs &quot;251 = 99+133+19&quot;; 99+134+19=252).</td><td>The 2.1.0 release note uses the self-consistent ledger: 251 = 99 L0 + 133 SVC + 19 built-ins; v1 baseline 233, all mapped (INTERFACES.md Part 1c).</td></tr><tr><td>P-03</td><td>P1 compatibility contract</td><td>The v1-&gt;v2 mapping table had 231 rows for a 233-command baseline: <code>health export</code> and <code>health watch</code> were silently dropped by a no-space parse regex.</td><td>Generator fixed; Part 1c maps all 233, zero MISSING.</td></tr><tr><td>P-04</td><td>P1 interface truth</td><td>No per-entry 162-&gt;158 comparison; &quot;register-class interfaces gone&quot; was an over-general summary.</td><td>INTERFACES.md Part 2c: the per-entry table over the complete v1 Part 2 truth (278 declarations: 267 public, 6 internalized, 5 gone); interfaces.json reports the current 17/158 surface + the v1 fate fields.</td></tr><tr><td>P-05</td><td>P1 deletions</td><td>No deletion register; the two real deletions were never declared.</td><td>INTERFACES.md Part 2d declares all five deletions (init/probe plumbing) with their verified v2 replacements; the register-class statement is exact.</td></tr><tr><td>P-06</td><td>P1 docs</td><td>ARCHITECTURE.md described the v1 tree (kernel/, userprogs/, 233, 53 syscalls, 162, &quot;boots into oc&gt; by default&quot;).</td><td>Rewritten for the real tree (two rings, 54 syscalls, 17/158, ush default entry), EN + zh mirrors.</td></tr><tr><td>P-07</td><td>P1 process docs</td><td>versioning.md (no +21 offset), process.md (WP- tags, v1 gates), dev/build.md, dev/e2e-suites.md, manual/index.md, BUILDING.md L141 all v1-era.</td><td>All updated to the v2 reality (v-tags, six-suite matrix + L1 per-app gate, +21 offset documented, current numbers).</td></tr><tr><td>P-08</td><td>P1 test coverage</td><td>No per-app L1 testing; the v1 28-check user-program regression battery was absent from the 2.0.0 matrix.</td><td>The 2.1.0 matrix adds the L1 per-app record (ush + every embedded program executed); see docs/dev/e2e-suites.md.</td></tr><tr><td>P-09</td><td>P1 comments</td><td>The Makefile comment claimed tui/chinese were &quot;rewritten as L1 ring-3 apps&quot; while both trees are still compiled INTO the kernel.</td><td>Comment corrected (tui/chinese stay L0-resident; l1apps/ holds ush + the embedded programs only).</td></tr><tr><td>P-10</td><td>P2 website data</td><td>site-data.js stats.lines=130,011 vs the real 130,495; update_website.sh copied the stale stats block forward.</td><td>update_website.sh REGENERATES the stats block from docs/data/*.json; site numbers always match the data files.</td></tr><tr><td>P-11</td><td>P2 assets</td><td>The public repo main tree did not hold the release ISO/SRC (only the private mirror + gh-pages did), contradicting the release note.</td><td>2.1.0 assets are committed to the public main (website/public/downloads/) byte-identical with the Release/gh-pages.</td></tr><tr><td>P-12</td><td>P2 assets</td><td>website/public/update*.json still carried WP-13/build-259 manifests (top-level pair was 2.0.0/302).</td><td>update_website.sh writes BOTH copies byte-identical (top level + public/).</td></tr><tr><td>P-13</td><td>P2 evidence</td><td>The five suite logs the 2.0.0 evidence README referenced did not exist in either repo; the SRC file name in asset_hashes.txt was wrong.</td><td>The five logs are real files regenerated by the 2.1.0 run (evidence/), and asset_hashes.txt names the actual published files.</td></tr><tr><td>P-14</td><td>P2 counting caliber</td><td>commands.json count_l1_svc=133 vs the 142 real ush SVC table rows - 9 dual-homed names silently attributed to L0; interfaces.json still carried the v1 162 official count.</td><td>Caliber stated exactly in commands.json + INTERFACES.md Part 1b (142 rows, 9 dual-homed, ledger 251 unchanged); interfaces.json reports 17/158.</td></tr><tr><td>P-15</td><td>P2 docs</td><td>docs/manual/commands was a v1 artifact (234 pages, &quot;Registered in: shell/...&quot; paths); the 23 v2-new commands had no pages.</td><td>Fully regenerated: 251 pages (EN + zh) with real v2 homes, ghost-page purge, help hints from l1svc_defs.h.</td></tr><tr><td>P-16</td><td>P2 governance</td><td>The private mirror&#39;s SYNC-RULES.md described a tree layout that no longer exists; the 2.0.0 work log was not in-repo.</td><td>SYNC-RULES.md rewritten for the current layout; WORK_LOG.md extended through 2.1.0 (both in the private mirror).</td></tr><tr><td>P-17</td><td>P3 text defects</td><td>A docs/dev/index.md link flagged as &quot;ost-tests (missing h)&quot;; the wp14_site_v2 evidence dir name; the 379-file caliber note.</td><td>The link defect turned out to be a FALSE POSITIVE (byte-level inspection shows <code>[host-tests]</code> was always correct - a display artifact had eaten the <code>[h</code> sequence in the postcheck evidence; recorded honestly). The evidence dir naming is corrected for 2.1.0 and the file-count caliber is documented in the data note.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="_2-1-0-open-issues-all-fixed-in-2-2-0" tabindex="-1">2.1.0 open issues - all fixed in 2.2.0 <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-1-0-open-issues-all-fixed-in-2-2-0" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2.1.0 open issues - all fixed in 2.2.0&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Class</th><th>Issue</th><th>Fixed in 2.2.0 by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>O-01</td><td>kernel</td><td>wp10_tui suite #DF after the closing checkupdate SVC round (2.0.0 and 2.1.0 identical; declared in the 2.1.0 delivery dossier). Root cause: the deepest real kernel call chain (SYS_L1SVC -&gt; ota_update_check -&gt; http_get_body 6000 B frame -&gt; net_tls_https_get 2144 -&gt; tls13_do_handshake -&gt; crypto_x509_verify_chain 1872 -&gt; crypto_rsa_verify_pss 2704 -&gt; crypto_bn_mod_exp 4160 -&gt; mont_mul 1200 -&gt; mont_redc 1856; -fstack-usage sum ~23.6 KB) plus a timer/e1000 IRQ nesting on the same stack exceeded the 20 KiB usable kthread stack (24 KiB alloc minus the unmapped guard page). Recorded crash: rip=crypto_bn_mont_redc+0x2A, rsp 20,880 B below the stack top inside the guard page.</td><td>OC_KTHREAD_STACK_PAGES 6 -&gt; 10 (40 KiB window / 36 KiB usable, ~8 KiB headroom over the measured worst chain + IRQ nesting; cost 512 KiB of the ~505 MiB PMM pool). The corrupted #DF panic banner (&quot;andled&quot; -&gt; &quot;handled&quot;) is fixed too. Verified: the exact crashing chunk (wp10_tui tail + ush SVC rounds incl. checkupdate over live HTTPS) now runs to &quot;12/12 checks passed&quot; with the real checkupdate report and no panic. Commit 4293e94.</td></tr><tr><td>O-02</td><td>kernel</td><td>Flaky wait4-vs-fork timing race (the 2.1.0 open item). Misdiagnosed at 2.1.0 as an interrupt-timing race; actually DETERMINISTIC bookkeeping: when a process died, its children&#39;s g_procs entries kept parent_tid = the dead tid; after the tid was freed and reused, the new task&#39;s wait4(any-child) collected a stale zombie&#39;s exit code and returned before its own child was scheduled (g6_exec_a printed &quot;child status = 142&quot; = 128+SIGSEGV of pf_test&#39;s #PF-killed unwaited child, verdict ahead of the child&#39;s first output). Reproduced pre-fix in a same-boot marathon AND in a fresh boot with the zombie sources ahead of the victim (2/2 boots).</td><td>Orphan-detach in user_process_reap_resources (every entry still pointing at the dying tid -&gt; sentinel -1, unownable by any task), plus the sys_fork half-product corpse paths (address-space copy failure / task creation failure) also detach - the living parent never collects a child POSIX says was never created. Verified: fresh-boot battery 20/20 boots CLEAN (pre-fix 2/2 hit the anomaly); same-boot marathon g6_exec_a = 138 both rounds; same-boot stress (zombie sources + wait4 family x 8 rounds = 80 runs) 0 anomalies, 8/8 status 138. Commit 11d2127.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="open-issues" tabindex="-1">Open issues <a class="header-anchor" href="#open-issues" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Open issues&quot;">​</a></h2><p>None known at 3.0.0-WP03. The WP-02 declared honest boundary (the OCOS side of the OCIRE embed was unimplemented: no oc.* fact parsing, no SIGNAL_OK, no watchdog disarm, no agent channel) is CLOSED by WP-03 G4 (<code>src/L0/services/ocembed/</code>, the supervised battery 3/3 in <code>evidence/3_0_0_wp03_g4_ocembed/</code>). Declared post-WP-03 limitations (by design, not defects):</p><ul><li>The ush <code>kill</code> reports &quot;no such process&quot; when a kill of the ocire_guard agent is REFUSED - the syscall ABI carries a single error code, so the refusal text is generic; the true reason is in the kernel log (klog WARN &quot;kill refused: ocire_guard process&quot;).</li><li>The pre-G5 bare <code>sti;hlt</code> park remains in four proven paths (sys_read&#39;s keyboard wait, the wp8cd fg wait, the sdhci timed wait, the OTA settle loop): the preempted-halt freeze it can cause was fixed for the power sleep (timer-callback + block) and never observed on those four; a real timed-block wakeup primitive is future scheduler work.</li><li>The direct-boot GRUB entry (&quot;Open Cube OS (direct boot)&quot;) carries no oc.* facts: a direct boot is unsupervised by definition (no signal, no protection) - the supervisor entry is the only supervised path.</li></ul><h2 id="_3-0-0-wp06g2-notes" tabindex="-1">3.0.0-WP06g2 notes <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-0-0-wp06g2-notes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.0.0-WP06g2 notes&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The WP06g1 close-out&#39;s &quot;make all does not rebuild userprogs&quot; gap (the 06g1 catch-2) is CLOSED by WP06g2: <code>src/L0/core/userprogs_data.h</code> is a real make target now (any L1 source, i18n resource, or generator script newer than the header regenerates it; <code>make all</code> and <code>make iso</code> both carry the prerequisite; the emit is idempotent - byte-compare before write, mtime kept when unchanged). Live-verified both ways: a marked app rides <code>make all</code> into the ISO byte-identical with the fresh build, and reverting the mark regenerates the original header.</p><p>Declared WP06g2 boundaries (honest limits, not defects):</p><ul><li>The L0 diagnostic/test-class service bodies (the *_test and wp-style programs&#39; captured outputs) keep their English technical text; the user-facing sentence class (usage/errors/descriptions of the file tools, the registry hints, the editors, the IME, the shell) is bilingual through the i18n resources. Column headers and state words deliberately stay English (the zh_CN locale convention - CJK width would break the alignment).</li><li>The OCSE install-wizard GUI text was already bilingual in WP-06c (its own dictionary); the TUI desktop pages remain English this batch.</li><li>The IME phrase library is 622 hand-authored high-frequency words; single characters cover all 3755 level-1 hanzi as before.</li></ul><p>WP06g2 delivery-fix addendum (post-close-out, same batch):</p><ul><li><p>The close-out push never reached a green CI: the phrase-library generator imported pypinyin, and the clean CI environment (no pip packages, by design) failed the ISO step with ModuleNotFoundError. The generator is now self-contained - the pinyin composes from pinyin_data.c&#39;s own single-char table (the syllables the engine itself matches), and the ten polyphonic words carry their dictionary readings as explicit pairs in the list; the emitted table is byte-identical to the pypinyin-built one. A misspelled explicit pair cannot pass the legal-syllable decomposition check.</p></li><li><p>The site-data.js the wp06g1 site carried had a stray <code>},</code> after the dev block - a SyntaxError, so window.SITE_DATA never loaded and the app.js fill layer (stats, download cards, history table) silently ran on the static HTML fallbacks since wp06g1. The structure is repaired and the data corrected in the same pass: the stable line is 2.2.0/316 again (the close-out had pushed the milestone into the stable slot, which would have pointed the OTA channel at a package that was never uploaded), and the dev block carries the real WP06g2 pair (build 403).</p></li><li><p>WP06g3 correction - the &quot;branches: ain]&quot; entry that used to ride here was a phantom. A byte-level audit (od -c against fef5f41, 0226e8c, and HEAD) shows the workflow filters have read <code>branches: [main]</code> since the day the file was created; the &quot;ain]&quot; reading was a terminal-transcription artifact (the shell-output display layer eats a literal &#39;[m&#39; as if it were an ANSI SGR reset sequence), never a real typo. No workflow change was ever needed; the entry is retired.</p></li><li><p>tools/run_fix4_regression.py (the v2.x-era 28-check battery) asserts the old boot flow (the <code>oc&gt;</code> L0 recovery-shell prompt, the v1.0.0 banner, &quot;233 commands&quot;) - the main ISO has booted straight to the L1 shell (<code>ush&gt;</code>) for many batches now, so the battery cannot pass as-is and the release SOP runs without --deep. It needs an assertion refresh; the wp06g3 batteries (serial_enc, ocse_enc, editor_rework, ocire_menu, install_ocire) and the https e2e 7/7 carry this batch&#39;s regression weight.</p></li></ul>',15)])])}const m=t(d,[["render",o]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
